ockSize` to return raw 512-byte blocks instead of 1K blocks, then all the accumulated values will be in 512-byte units, and I'll convert them at display time. That should handle the math correctly.\n\nFor the `-x` flag, I should verify that GNU du completely skips mount point directories rather than counting them, since that affects how I handle the traversal. we print anything? GNU du does NOT print anything for skipped directories. So returning 0 without printing is correct.\n\nThere's a subtle point here though: when we skip a cross-device directory, we're not counting its blocks, but the mount point itself is on the mounted filesystem, so its block count shouldn't be included anyway. Returning 0 handles this correctly.\n\nFor the `-s` and `-d` interaction, I should set `maxDepth` to 0 when summarize mode is enabled, since `-s` is equivalent to `--max-depth=0`. But I need to be careful because `shouldPrint` already handles `-s` separately, so I need to think through how these interact.\n\nActually, I could simplify by converting `-s` to `maxDepth=0` during parsing and then just checking `maxDepth` in `shouldPrint`. But the current code already works, so I'll keep it straightforward and add the maxDepth check alongside the existing summarize logic.\n\nUpdating `shouldPrint`... I'm verifying the flag interactions work correctly—when `-s` is set it overrides `-d` to only show depth 0, matching GNU du's behavior. The `kFlag` variable is passed by address to the flag package so it won't trigger linter warnings even though we don't read its value.\n\nNow I'm planning the edits to cmd/du/main.go: updating the package comment, adding new fields to the options struct, and modifying the flag parsing logic.\n\nWriting the refactored Go file... Setting up the signal handler, then parsing command-line flags for options like summarize, all files, and grand total, before processing each argument path and accumulating the total disk usage across them. I'm defining the function t